    "knowledge_point_id": "ebd2a5c8-5d15-589d-81c9-9b67f4e8f0b3",
    "knowledge_point": "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve Containing Special Functions",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031229/assets/59704c6b8e643337113ab99b22eaeaac8d59ac3c15042409e8a0a8eac6ee54fd.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=\\frac{t}{2},\\,y={e}^{t}$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=1$ (shown above)?",
    "worked_solution": "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{({e}^{2t})+(\\frac{1}{4})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{4{e}^{2t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
